WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.500
There is a play
by one of my favorite writers,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.060
Nobel laureate Wole Soyinka,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.620
I would like to tell you about,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.300
"The Swamp Dwellers."

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.780
"The Swamp Dwellers"
are about a blind beggar

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.540
who attempts to buy a piece of swampland

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.900
because he believes
that the swamps will provide him

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.380
with a better livelihood.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.220
However, the villagers
in that swamp believe

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.820
that deep in the forests
of the swamp lies a deity

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.420
whose job is to protect them
from all misfortune.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.620
There is another swamp

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.220
that protects us from misfortune.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.620
And holds the promise
of humanity's survival.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:55.220
Those are the peatlands
of the Congo Basin.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.580
The peatlands of the Congo Basin

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:05.980
are a lush, vast, expanse of weeds, shrubs

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.340
and trees that absorb carbon

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.820
from the atmosphere.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:16.820
They span 145,000 kilometers.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.500
The peat formations
began 10,000 years ago,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.100
when man moved from
hunter-gatherer to farmer.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.340
Nature, I guess, figured,
"I better start protecting myself."

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.140
A couple of years ago,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.900
scientists discovered
that these tropical peatlands

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.900
are probably the largest
carbon sink in the world.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.740
They store 30 billion tons of carbon.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.940
If they did not exist,

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.180
we would have probably
already stepped beyond

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.140
the threshold of 1.5 degrees of warming.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.820
However, the peatlands are under threat.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:16.100
We have, of course,
oil explorations being contemplated.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.900
Logging licenses
have already been awarded.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.420
And many of the communities
that live around the peatlands

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.940
have just this for survival.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.460
We have all collectively seen,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.380
in the Amazon forest,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.020
burning down because of economic interest.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:44.420
Turning this forest
from carbon sink to carbon source.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:51.900
We have witnessed, in Indonesia,
the swamps drained

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.980
for palm oil plantations.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.020
Turning them from carbon sink
to carbon source.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.700
It will be devastating if the same fate

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.740
were to befall the peatlands
of the Congo Basin.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.500
The peatlands are a passion of mine.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.540
I believe they must be protected.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.140
But we must also, in this fight
against climate change,

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.980
ensure that responsibility
and accountability

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.180
are adequately apportioned.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.140
The world emits over
54 billion tons of carbon.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.740
The peatlands of the Congo Basin
absorb a large majority of that.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:47.820
Africa emits 1.2 billion
tons of carbon a year.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:54.460
And the communities
that live around the peatlands

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.700
have no access to basic services.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.420
Education, health care,

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:06.140
clean drinking water, an adequate meal,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.340
good transportation, technology.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.820
Clearly, they are not tweeting.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:19.060
The peatlands are an economic asset,

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:25.300
which, if valued appropriately,
could provide the stepping stone

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:29.380
for these communities
to improve their livelihoods.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:36.700
Nature has acted already
as a store of value for the peatlands.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.900
A kind of federal reserve.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.180
Now, imagine if we had a price
for carbon at 50 dollars.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:52.420
With 30 billion tons of carbon
sequestered in the peatlands,

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.220
we're talking at 1.5 trillion.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.180
A peatland development fund

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.820
could help us today
to address the stubborn

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:09.980
and seemingly persistent
developmental challenges

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.220
that the continent faces today.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.900
This is not just about decarbonization.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.100
This is also about
development with dignity.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.180
This is about equity.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.500
It is about justice,
and it is about transparency.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:38.660
(Applause)

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:44.060
Indeed, Africa has not
contributed enormously

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.780
to the climate crisis.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:53.300
But we have visibly contributed enormously
to keeping the planet alive.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:56.380
We can, therefore ...

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.740
(Applause)

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.020
Not expect, but as the blind beggar,

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:10.860
we continue to plead for assistance
to achieve a better livelihood,

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:17.580
and we certainly cannot be like
Wole Soyinka’s swamp dwellers

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:21.780
who wait patiently
while the world overlooks

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:26.380
and undermines Africa's contribution
to climate change.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:32.660
Science has recognized
Africa's contribution.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:38.580
I ask you to join me
in recognizing Africa's contribution,

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:40.100
and collectively,

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:44.340
we must ask the markets to recognize

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:48.260
and adequately reward
Africa's contribution

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:51.180
by putting a price on carbon.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:55.660
(Applause)

